The differences of PostGIS's core regression tests that test/corpus.sh keeps,
reviewed (2026-10-06, PostGIS 3.7.0rc2 at e2ea03ad0bf, vexec on pg_accel's
vk branch), one file a session and test: exactly the difference the run
compares, between off's answer -- every core test passes against PostGIS's
expected output with vexec.mode = off -- and the session's.

Every one is a plan's node name, and nothing else: PostGIS's index tests
(regress_index, regress_brin_index, regress_brin_index_3d,
regress_brin_index_geography, regress_gist_index_nd, regress_spgist_index_2d,
regress_spgist_index_3d, regress_spgist_index_nd, temporal_knn) print the
node type of each query's plan beside its count of rows, and in force mode
vexec's vector scans stand where PostgreSQL's scans stood: "Seq Scan" is
"Vec Seq Scan", "Bitmap Heap Scan" is "Vec Bitmap Heap Scan".  The counts on
the same lines are off's.  They are the same with the pack preloaded and
without it, in both batch formats.
